1366
01:07:26,030 --> 01:07:26,790
Minus one.

1367
01:07:28,810 --> 01:07:29,370
Right?

1368
01:07:32,570 --> 01:07:33,750
But what is it saying?

1369
01:07:34,010 --> 01:07:35,090
Why is it saying that?

1370
01:07:43,000 --> 01:07:44,440
Is it not just zero?

1371
01:07:44,760 --> 01:07:45,360
Hold on.

1372
01:07:45,480 --> 01:07:46,600
We were actually talking right now.

1373
01:07:47,580 --> 01:07:49,300
The meteor is graphing something.

1374
01:07:51,040 --> 01:07:51,600
Meteorite.

1375
01:07:53,180 --> 01:07:53,860
Shove it.

1376
01:07:54,720 --> 01:07:56,180
Team, how do we respond?

1377
01:07:57,060 --> 01:07:59,440
The first three signals tells us how to continue.

1378
01:08:00,020 --> 01:08:00,920
Like last transmission.

1379
01:08:02,420 --> 01:08:03,800
Question mark equals zero.

1380
01:08:04,540 --> 01:08:06,560
I hate not understanding a transmission.

1381
01:08:07,680 --> 01:08:08,460
Me too.

1382
01:08:08,780 --> 01:08:10,500
But the meteorite's given us the go-ahead, right?

1383
01:08:12,540 --> 01:08:15,040
Question mark equals zero must be their way of saying, who cares?

1384
01:08:15,360 --> 01:08:15,760
Carry on.

1385
01:08:16,800 --> 01:08:19,960
I suppose, but why include a transmission to skip in the first place?

1386
01:08:20,700 --> 01:08:21,380
Who knows?

1387
01:08:22,260 --> 01:08:23,320
No, no, no, no, no, no, no.

1388
01:08:23,380 --> 01:08:24,240
There's something about this.

1389
01:08:26,200 --> 01:08:27,200
There's something about this.

1390
01:08:27,860 --> 01:08:29,220
Like, why would they give us all this?

1391
01:08:30,780 --> 01:08:34,480
Maybe they... Oh, okay, so if you actually graph this...

1392
01:08:34,950 --> 01:08:36,450
And you graph all of these functions.

1393
01:08:36,630 --> 01:08:38,450
Maybe they intersect in some funny way.

1394
01:08:39,070 --> 01:08:41,150
Maybe it's gonna draw something funny to us.

1395
01:08:41,950 --> 01:08:42,470
You know?

1396
01:08:43,310 --> 01:08:43,970
I don't know.

1397
01:08:44,170 --> 01:08:45,410
But, I mean, answer is zero.

1398
01:08:48,970 --> 01:08:49,370
Yeah.

1399
01:08:51,410 --> 01:08:51,990
Oh, okay.

1400
01:08:52,070 --> 01:08:52,390
That was that.

1401
01:08:52,570 --> 01:08:52,830
Nice.

1402
01:08:54,470 --> 01:08:55,910
No, just when the music starts.

1403
01:08:55,910 --> 01:08:56,650
That's too bad.

1404
01:09:02,100 --> 01:09:03,420
What the hell?

1405
01:09:06,300 --> 01:09:08,820
What is that growth coming out of it?

1406
01:09:08,940 --> 01:09:09,920
That's nasty.

1407
01:09:11,600 --> 01:09:12,240
That's it.

1408
01:09:12,720 --> 01:09:16,860
This rock is our window into a world completely foreign to this planet.

1409
01:09:20,140 --> 01:09:24,540
Apart from the right side with the transmitter, it looks like any old earth rock.

1410
01:09:25,260 --> 01:09:26,140
No, it doesn't.

1411
01:09:26,440 --> 01:09:28,180
What about the veins on the rock?

1412
01:09:32,000 --> 01:09:33,660
That's exactly what I said.

1413
01:09:33,900 --> 01:09:34,740
I said what?

1414
01:09:34,880 --> 01:09:35,600
No, it doesn't.

1415
01:09:36,880 --> 01:09:38,220
It's got a fusion crust.

1416
01:09:38,680 --> 01:09:39,420
That dark exterior.

1417
01:09:39,660 --> 01:09:40,380
Okay, that's not what I said.

1418
01:09:40,460 --> 01:09:41,340
What about the veins, though?

1419
01:09:41,440 --> 01:09:42,060
Look at the veins.

1420
01:09:43,800 --> 01:09:47,160
That's the shell created by the meteor melting as it entered our atmosphere.

1421
01:09:48,400 --> 01:09:51,800
This is the last layer that didn't burn off, so it cools to form a crust.

1422
01:09:52,940 --> 01:09:55,040
I don't see that in any old earth rock.

1423
01:09:56,540 --> 01:09:57,840
It could be volcanic, no?

1424
01:09:58,960 --> 01:09:59,760
Absolutely not.

1425
01:10:00,140 --> 01:10:01,760
You see those dimples all over the rock?

1426
01:10:02,760 --> 01:10:07,700
Those are arachmaclypts, where bubbles of molten rock carve divots on atmospheric entry.

1427
01:10:08,580 --> 01:10:08,980
Fine.

1428
01:10:09,900 --> 01:10:11,900
This thing walks and talks like a meteorite.

1429
01:10:12,440 --> 01:10:13,840
It's even got the meteorite smell.

1430
01:10:14,800 --> 01:10:17,080
Sorry, Dr. Akers, you can smell meteors?

1431
01:10:17,960 --> 01:10:19,440
Uh, no, I made that part up.

1432
01:10:19,880 --> 01:10:21,020
It'd be cool if I could, though.

1433
01:10:22,120 --> 01:10:23,400
There is a computer in there.

1434
01:10:23,940 --> 01:10:24,420
Yep.

1435
01:10:24,860 --> 01:10:27,380
Something in there is sending and listening for radio waves.

1436
01:10:28,160 --> 01:10:31,360
We haven't cracked it open because we'd like to avoid breaking said computer.

1437
01:10:32,060 --> 01:10:32,340
Good.

1438
01:10:32,880 --> 01:10:35,220
Yet you managed to chip it during transport?

1439
01:10:36,560 --> 01:10:39,960
Still, we've taken samples of the rock and conducted non-destructive measurements.

1440
01:10:40,920 --> 01:10:44,620
Though it is killing me to know how the computer in there isn't smashed from the landing.

1441
01:10:45,500 --> 01:10:48,320
The internal material they invented to absorb that impact.

1442
01:10:48,600 --> 01:10:50,400
Man, I'd love to get my hands on that.

1443
01:10:53,380 --> 01:10:53,860
Why?

1444
01:10:53,860 --> 01:10:55,020
Why you specifically?

1445
01:10:55,200 --> 01:10:56,620
Why would you like to get your hands on it?

1446
01:10:57,700 --> 01:10:59,300
Just imagine the applications.

1447
01:11:00,380 --> 01:11:01,920
You said you're collecting samples.

1448
01:11:03,260 --> 01:11:05,980
I'm seeing chondruels in the cleaved portion on the left there.

1449
01:11:06,700 --> 01:11:07,980
How dense is this rock?

1450
01:11:08,900 --> 01:11:12,300
We're finding it to be around 3 grams per cubic centimeter at most sites.

1451
01:11:13,180 --> 01:11:14,960
And when measured by a specific gravity?

1452
01:11:15,580 --> 01:11:17,380
3.12 from our best reading.

1453
01:11:18,570 --> 01:11:20,830
Walks like a chondrite, quacks like a chondrite.

1454
01:11:21,010 --> 01:11:21,950
Must be a chondrite.

1455
01:11:22,750 --> 01:11:24,790
Tell me, how much iron is baked into this beauty?

1456
01:11:25,650 --> 01:11:26,290
Fairly low.

1457
01:11:26,550 --> 01:11:27,410
It's pretty lightweight.

1458
01:11:28,290 --> 01:11:28,590
Nickel?

1459
01:11:29,370 --> 01:11:30,190
About the same.

1460
01:11:31,030 --> 01:11:31,850
Help me follow.

1461
01:11:32,610 --> 01:11:35,110
We call it a chondrite because it's got chondrules.

1462
01:11:35,330 --> 01:11:36,390
Tiny rocky grains.

1463
01:11:37,170 --> 01:11:39,910
Which means that no geologic activity has transformed it.

1464
01:11:40,670 --> 01:11:42,930
It likely wasn't ever part of a planet or a moon.

1465
01:11:43,670 --> 01:11:45,770
It's pretty typical as far as space rocks go.

1466
01:11:46,540 --> 01:11:48,700
Well, aside from the alien radio transmitter.

1467
01:11:49,020 --> 01:11:50,680
And what about the veins?

1468
01:11:51,680 --> 01:11:52,880
Talk about the veins!

1469
01:11:53,880 --> 01:11:55,280
Walk me through the iron and nickel.

1470
01:11:55,540 --> 01:11:56,380
What's that tell us?

1471
01:11:57,700 --> 01:12:00,320
If it's this low in iron and nickel, it's an oldie.

1472
01:12:01,280 --> 01:12:05,120
Stars fuse atoms, starting from hydrogen and helium to make heavier and heavier atoms.

1473
01:12:06,120 --> 01:12:08,120
Eventually, we get to heavier elements.

1474
01:12:08,360 --> 01:12:08,860
Think iron.

1475
01:12:09,220 --> 01:12:09,700
Platinum.

1476
01:12:10,030 --> 01:12:10,830
Traditional metals.

1477
01:12:11,470 --> 01:12:14,590
Then stars explode, run out of fuel, collapse, whatever.

1478
01:12:15,450 --> 01:12:18,750
Expelling their elements somewhere else, only for it to come along and make a new star.

1479
01:12:19,290 --> 01:12:20,270
Now here's the trick.

1480
01:12:20,770 --> 01:12:24,830
The new star takes those heavier atoms to start, and makes even heavier elements.

1481
01:12:25,750 --> 01:12:30,650
So each step, younger stars make heavier atoms, which then form heavier rocks.

1482
01:12:31,650 --> 01:12:36,190
So if we're low on iron, it means we're looking at an early universe primitive.

1483
01:12:37,110 --> 01:12:38,950
This body's been floating around a while.

1484
01:12:39,730 --> 01:12:43,530
Until something, someone, made it an interstellar carrier pigeon.

1485
01:12:44,290 --> 01:12:45,850
And that pigeon flew to us.

1486
01:12:46,590 --> 01:12:47,510
What are the odds?

1487
01:12:48,450 --> 01:12:49,850
I can't imagine the odds.

1488
01:12:50,590 --> 01:12:53,850
Over a month on this mission and it remains simply incomprehensible for me.

1489
01:12:54,670 --> 01:12:56,110
Oh yeah, it's been a month already?

1490
01:12:56,270 --> 01:12:56,870
I guess so.

1491
01:12:58,510 --> 01:13:00,110
Every one of these is like a week, right?

1492
01:13:00,930 --> 01:13:02,230
Outer space gets like that.

1493
01:13:02,910 --> 01:13:03,390
Incomprehensible.

1494
01:13:04,510 --> 01:13:09,890
Whatever the odds are, however, incomprehensible it may seem, we received this message, this gift.

1495
01:13:10,510 --> 01:13:11,990
I can't help but feel gratitude.

1496
01:13:14,530 --> 01:13:17,550
Translation team, I'm glad you all could finally see the meteor for yourselves.

1497
01:13:19,050 --> 01:13:20,550
Ooh, that's kind of creepy.

1498
01:13:21,590 --> 01:13:23,490
Dude, that growth looked disgusting.

1499
01:13:24,150 --> 01:13:29,430
And I will say, though, that antenna looked oddly human in design.

1500
01:13:30,750 --> 01:13:32,250
So, what's going on there?

1501
01:13:32,780 --> 01:13:33,340
You know?

1502
01:13:34,240 --> 01:13:35,020
No clue?

1503
01:13:36,360 --> 01:13:37,380
Groups completed.

1504
01:13:37,720 --> 01:13:38,540
No clue.

1505
01:13:39,940 --> 01:13:40,540
What?

1506
01:13:41,120 --> 01:13:42,280
What do you mean, no clue?

1507
01:13:43,740 --> 01:13:44,500
Alan's journal.

1508
01:13:45,120 --> 01:13:45,400
Status.

1509
01:13:45,740 --> 01:13:45,880
Aw.

1510
01:13:46,480 --> 01:13:48,200
It sometimes slips my mind, what we're doing.

1511
01:13:48,780 --> 01:13:51,280
Setting eyes on the meteorite for myself helped remind me.

1512
01:13:52,640 --> 01:13:53,240
Bautista.

1513
01:13:53,380 --> 01:13:54,560
There was a computer in that rock.

1514
01:13:54,800 --> 01:13:55,660
Somewhere inside.

1515
01:13:57,600 --> 01:13:59,340
This guy loves computers.

1516
01:13:59,930 --> 01:14:02,870
He doesn't care about the fact that it's freaking coming from deep space.

1517
01:14:03,010 --> 01:14:03,850
It's aliens and shit.

1518
01:14:03,990 --> 01:14:06,770
He's like... I need to see what kind of processor they got in there.

1519
01:14:07,310 --> 01:14:08,130
Oh my god.

1520
01:14:08,630 --> 01:14:09,730
Look at all that RAM.

1521
01:14:10,610 --> 01:14:12,810
It's even got... DDR6.

1522
01:14:14,930 --> 01:14:15,590
Carrie's diary.

1523
01:14:16,090 --> 01:14:18,010
Dear diary, I finally laid eyes on the meteor.

1524
01:14:18,910 --> 01:14:22,490
I had been on the other side of the wall, trying to make sense of its every utterance.

1525
01:14:22,690 --> 01:14:23,770
Its every transmission.

1526
01:14:24,130 --> 01:14:25,130
Its every frequency.

1527
01:14:25,590 --> 01:14:27,230
Now I have seen the meteor herself.

1528
01:14:27,690 --> 01:14:31,090
Our only window to a civilization residing somewhere deep in space.

1529
01:14:31,910 --> 01:14:32,470
Beautiful.

1530
01:14:33,990 --> 01:14:34,790
And Doppler.

1531
01:14:35,090 --> 01:14:38,070
Been working with the materials team on the Meteor for the past six weeks.

1532
01:14:38,330 --> 01:14:40,330
Almost grew numb to how remarkable it is.

1533
01:14:40,670 --> 01:14:43,110
I'm glad I was finally able to show the translation team.

1534
01:14:43,330 --> 01:14:45,290
They've helped remind me what it's all about.

1535
01:14:47,130 --> 01:14:48,210
And my thoughts.

1536
01:14:49,770 --> 01:14:52,570
That Meteor was creepy.

1537
01:14:53,470 --> 01:14:55,450
I did not like...

1538
01:14:55,840 --> 01:15:00,580
The organic growth on the right side of it.

1539
01:15:03,520 --> 01:15:08,740
Especially the way it was fused with electronics.

1540
01:15:10,480 --> 01:15:11,680
With tech.

1541
01:15:13,020 --> 01:15:13,660
Yeah.

1542
01:15:14,580 --> 01:15:15,680
Those are my thoughts.

1543
01:15:16,960 --> 01:15:18,740
What if they're here to kill us all?

1544
01:15:19,480 --> 01:15:20,080
You know?

1545
01:15:20,600 --> 01:15:22,320
Man, we're gonna solve this whole thing.

1546
01:15:22,460 --> 01:15:23,860
We're gonna, you know...

1547
01:15:23,900 --> 01:15:25,460
Get to the end of these transmissions.

1548
01:15:26,160 --> 01:15:32,920
And then when the aliens have confirmed that... Yep, we have reached a... specific level of intelligence.

1549
01:15:32,920 --> 01:15:33,800
They're gonna wipe us out.

1550
01:15:34,380 --> 01:15:35,680
It's gonna explode or something.

1551
01:15:35,780 --> 01:15:36,500
And we're all gonna die.

1552
01:15:38,980 --> 01:15:40,480
Act 1 complete!

1553
01:15:41,620 --> 01:15:43,260
Math, our common bond.

1554
01:15:43,760 --> 01:15:45,000
Act 2 begins.

1555
01:15:45,640 --> 01:15:46,680
Oh, shit!

1556
01:15:48,040 --> 01:15:49,760
So we're gonna get something other than math?

1557
01:15:51,430 --> 01:15:52,170
Or what?

1558
01:15:54,890 --> 01:15:56,550
I want to know about the aliens.

1559
01:15:58,450 --> 01:15:59,610
Oh, it's still math.

1560
01:16:00,650 --> 01:16:01,130
What?

1561
01:16:01,510 --> 01:16:02,790
And there's two new signals.

1562
01:16:03,710 --> 01:16:04,350
Oh, man.

1563
01:16:04,410 --> 01:16:04,950
I don't know.

1564
01:16:06,530 --> 01:16:11,450
I mean, math is cool, but... it's definitely a little tedious so far.

1565
01:16:12,770 --> 01:16:15,610
But we are in Act 2, so maybe that will change now.

1566
01:16:16,570 --> 01:16:17,450
I'll see you next time.
